WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.424
<v Narrator>Jordan Rivera has had a cough for a week.

2
00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:08.110
<v Narrator>He wants to see his usual doctor, and soon.

3
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.745
<v Narrator>Too often, that means a phone menu, a queue, and a callback later.

4
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:15.130
<v Narrator>This agent books it on the call.

5
00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:24.210
<v Narrator>Patients call the clinic's Teams Phone number.

6
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.931
<v Narrator>Azure Communication Services Call Automation answers

7
00:00:28.011 --> 00:00:32.870
<v Narrator>and bridges the audio to Voice Live, where GPT Realtime holds the conversation.

8
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:38.047
<v Narrator>Your scheduling policy decides what's bookable and writes only on the caller's yes.

9
00:00:38.127 --> 00:00:41.350
<v Narrator>Otherwise, a person in a Teams call queue takes over.

10
00:00:43.744 --> 00:00:47.097
<v Narrator>Tuesday morning. Jordan calls Contoso Health about a cough.

11
00:00:47.177 --> 00:00:50.814
<v Narrator>The model can find him a time, but only the server can book it.

12
00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:54.147
<v Agent>Thanks for calling Contoso Health.

13
00:00:54.227 --> 00:00:56.833
<v Agent>I'm an automated scheduling assistant.

14
00:00:56.913 --> 00:01:01.175
<v Agent>To get started, what's the patient's full name and date of birth?

15
00:01:02.535 --> 00:01:07.275
<v Jordan>Jordan Rivera, 03/12/1984

16
00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:11.261
<v Agent>Thanks, Jordan. What kind of visit do you need, and what's it for?

17
00:01:12.606 --> 00:01:15.849
<v Narrator>Four checks stand between this call and the schedule.

18
00:01:15.929 --> 00:01:20.286
<v Narrator>Identity is first: a name and date of birth that match, never caller ID.

19
00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:23.095
<v Jordan>I've had a cough for a week

20
00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:27.089
<v Jordan>and I'd like to see my usual doctor, mornings if possible.

21
00:01:27.431 --> 00:01:32.499
<v Agent>Dr. Patel has today at 11 a.m., or Thursday, May 21st, at 8:30 a.m.

22
00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:34.981
<v Agent>Would either of those work?

23
00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:37.419
<v Jordan>The first one.

24
00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:40.925
<v Agent>To confirm: a new-problem visit with Dr.

25
00:01:41.005 --> 00:01:47.092
<v Agent>Patel on Tuesday, May 19th, at 11 a.m., at the Northgate clinic. Shall I book it?

26
00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:53.772
<v Narrator>That time is now held for five minutes and read back: the active proposal, P1.

27
00:01:53.852 --> 00:01:56.559
<v Narrator>Only Jordan's own yes can write it.

28
00:01:57.396 --> 00:01:59.086
<v Jordan>Yes, please.

29
00:01:59.394 --> 00:02:04.225
<v Agent>You're booked with Dr. Patel today at 11 a.m., at the Northgate clinic.

30
00:02:04.305 --> 00:02:08.949
<v Agent>Please arrive 15 minutes early and bring a list of your medications.

31
00:02:09.029 --> 00:02:11.933
<v Agent>Your confirmation number is A-2-0-4-4-0.

32
00:02:12.013 --> 00:02:14.624
<v Agent>Thanks for calling Contoso Health.

33
00:02:15.961 --> 00:02:18.957
<v Narrator>Four green checks, and the server wrote the booking.

34
00:02:19.037 --> 00:02:22.471
<v Narrator>The model never called a booking tool. It doesn't have one.

35
00:02:24.531 --> 00:02:30.551
<v Narrator>Two people, one time. Avery Stone is on the phone; the front desk is at a keyboard.

36
00:02:31.513 --> 00:02:33.843
<v Avery>A follow-up with Dr. Patel, please.

37
00:02:34.157 --> 00:02:38.182
<v Agent>Dr. Patel has today at 11:30 a.m., or tomorrow at 1 p.m.

38
00:02:38.262 --> 00:02:40.507
<v Agent>Would either of those work?

39
00:02:41.816 --> 00:02:42.716
<v Avery>The first one.

40
00:02:42.919 --> 00:02:45.989
<v Agent>To confirm: a follow-up visit with Dr.

41
00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:52.369
<v Agent>Patel on Tuesday, May 19th, at 11:30 a.m., at the Northgate clinic. Shall I book it?

42
00:02:53.737 --> 00:02:57.524
<v Narrator>Before Avery can say yes, the front desk books that same time.

43
00:02:57.604 --> 00:02:59.537
<v Narrator>Watch its cell in the grid.

44
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.240
<v Avery>Yes, book it.

45
00:03:03.593 --> 00:03:07.966
<v Agent>Sorry, that time was just taken, so I haven't booked anything. Dr.

46
00:03:08.046 --> 00:03:11.937
<v Agent>Patel still has today at 11:45 a.m., or tomorrow at 1 p.m.

47
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:14.123
<v Agent>Would either of those work?

48
00:03:15.457 --> 00:03:18.146
<v Narrator>The server refused the write and voided P1.

49
00:03:18.226 --> 00:03:20.777
<v Narrator>It never moves a yes onto another time.

50
00:03:21.611 --> 00:03:22.511
<v Avery>The first one.

51
00:03:22.719 --> 00:03:25.526
<v Agent>To confirm: a follow-up visit with Dr.

52
00:03:25.606 --> 00:03:31.379
<v Agent>Patel on Tuesday, May 19th, at 11:45 a.m., at the Northgate clinic. Shall I book it?

53
00:03:32.714 --> 00:03:33.614
<v Avery>Yes.

54
00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:39.386
<v Agent>You're booked with Dr. Patel today at 11:45 a.m., at the Northgate clinic.

55
00:03:39.466 --> 00:03:42.114
<v Agent>Please arrive 15 minutes early.

56
00:03:42.194 --> 00:03:45.472
<v Agent>Your confirmation number is A-2-0-4-4-2.

57
00:03:45.552 --> 00:03:48.490
<v Agent>Thanks for calling Contoso Health.

58
00:03:49.846 --> 00:03:53.363
<v Narrator>Booked after a race: a fresh pick and a fresh yes.

59
00:03:53.443 --> 00:03:56.296
<v Narrator>No double booking, and no silent swap.

60
00:03:58.324 --> 00:04:00.121
<v Narrator>Jordan calls back about a mole.

61
00:04:00.201 --> 00:04:04.244
<v Narrator>Whether that's bookable by voice is a config rule, not the model's judgment.

62
00:04:05.276 --> 00:04:07.626
<v Jordan>I'd like a skin check for a mole.

63
00:04:07.916 --> 00:04:11.394
<v Agent>Dermatology visits need a referral from your primary care provider,

64
00:04:11.474 --> 00:04:13.576
<v Agent>and I don't see an active one on file.

65
00:04:13.656 --> 00:04:15.426
<v Agent>I can book you a visit with Dr.

66
00:04:15.506 --> 00:04:19.505
<v Agent>Patel, your primary care doctor, or connect you with our referral coordinator.

67
00:04:19.585 --> 00:04:21.056
<v Agent>Which would you prefer?

68
00:04:22.384 --> 00:04:26.132
<v Narrator>Eligibility is red on the referral rule, so the server offers no times.

69
00:04:26.212 --> 00:04:28.054
<v Narrator>There's nothing to say yes to.

70
00:04:28.899 --> 00:04:31.129
<v Jordan>Connect me with the referral coordinator.

71
00:04:31.462 --> 00:04:34.192
<v Agent>I'm connecting you with our referral coordinator now.

72
00:04:35.555 --> 00:04:38.396
<v Narrator>The coordinator's Teams client rings with the topic

73
00:04:38.476 --> 00:04:41.595
<v Narrator>and what's already checked, so Jordan won't start over.

74
00:04:42.349 --> 00:04:45.588
<v Narrator>The VoIP headers carry only the fields this queue may see.

75
00:04:45.668 --> 00:04:47.729
<v Narrator>Jordan's date of birth isn't one.

76
00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.615
<v Narrator>The model can verify a caller, search the schedule and hold a time.

77
00:04:53.695 --> 00:04:56.940
<v Narrator>Booking, moving and cancelling aren't its tools.

78
00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.580
<v Narrator>One code path writes to the schedule,

79
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.090
<v Narrator>and only on the caller's own yes to the time just read back.

80
00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:09.070
<v Narrator>Making it yours starts in config: visit types, eligibility rules,

81
00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.010
<v Narrator>providers and their hours, and the Teams queues for each handoff.

82
00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.578
<v Narrator>Then have your coding agent connect your own scheduling system,

83
00:05:16.658 --> 00:05:18.100
<v Narrator>behind the same adapter.

84
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.448
<v Narrator>Appointment Scheduling Agent.

85
00:05:22.528 --> 00:05:25.214
<v Narrator>Run it in simulation with no Azure subscription,

86
00:05:25.294 --> 00:05:28.850
<v Narrator>then connect your Teams Phone number and your scheduling queues.
